THOUGHT: NullAway complains because `this.stack` (and thus `stackTop`) is considered possibly null, so `stackTop.parent` is an unsafe dereference. The method should either guarantee non-null or explicitly handle null. Given the usage, `root()` should likely throw if the stack is empty. I’ll update `root()` to check for null first and throw an IllegalStateException if `stackTop` is null, then keep the existing parent check. After editing, I’ll re-run the compile to confirm the NullAway error count drops by one and no new errors appear.

```bash
cd /home/vscode/nullness-benchmarks/gson && sed -i '788,794c\    Node<K, V> root() {
      Node<K, V> stackTop = this.stack;
      if (stackTop == null || stackTop.parent != null) {
        throw new IllegalStateException();
      }
      return stackTop;
    }' gson/src/main/java/com/google/gson/internal/LinkedHashTreeMap.java && ./gradlew :gson:compileJava -x test -q > new_build_output.log 2>&1 || true && grep -n "LinkedHashTreeMap" new_build_output.log && grep "NullAway" new_build_output.log | tail
```

